As can be seen, you have a lot of fine-grained control over the look of a nanoplot.

## Making nanoplots with bars using `plot_type="bar"`

We don't just support line plots in `~~GT.fmt_nanoplot()`, we also have the option to show bar
plots. The only thing you need to change is the value of `plot_type=` argument to `"bar"`.

Choosing between line and bar nanoplots depends on what you want to emphasize. Line plots highlight
trends and continuity over time or a sequence. And they make it easy to see whether values are
rising, falling, or oscillating. Bar plots, on the other hand, emphasize individual values and make
it straightforward to compare magnitudes across positions. Bar plots also handle negative values
more naturally, with bars projecting downward from a zero line, giving the reader an immediate sense
of sign and scale.

::: {#25b6ebcf .cell execution_count=6}
``` {.python .cell-code}
GT(random_numbers_df).fmt_nanoplot(columns="numbers", plot_type="bar")
```

